description:
Overlapped reads give every operation its own 64-bit offset and finish in any order (lesson 7.03). Write the two helpers that make that manageable.

```cpp
struct Overlapped { std::uint32_t Offset; std::uint32_t OffsetHigh; };

void setOffset(Overlapped& ov, std::uint64_t position);
std::uint64_t offsetOf(const Overlapped& ov);

class ChunkAssembler
{
public:
    explicit ChunkAssembler(std::uint64_t start = 0);
    bool add(std::uint64_t offset, std::string data);
    std::string takeReady();
    std::uint64_t position() const;
    std::size_t pending() const;
};
```

- **setOffset** stores the low 32 bits of `position` in `Offset` and the high 32 bits in `OffsetHigh`; **offsetOf** puts them back together.
- **ChunkAssembler** receives the data of completed reads in any order and hands it out **in file order**. `position` is the offset of the next byte to hand out (it starts at `start`).
- **add** stores a chunk. It returns `false` and stores nothing if the chunk is empty, starts before `position`, or overlaps a chunk already stored.
- **takeReady** returns all the bytes that are now contiguous from `position` (joining as many stored chunks as follow each other), removes them, and advances `position`. If the next chunk has not arrived, it returns an empty string.
- **pending** is the number of chunks stored and not yet handed out.

